      senior: 'Open the capstone course on the metric, because every practice in it is downstream of one sentence: "Moreover it is required in stewards, that a man be found faithful" (1 Corinthians 4:2). Teach what that rules out. It rules out yield as the primary measure, which is the measure the trade actually uses. A manager can produce a strong year through a rising market and be unfaithful throughout it; another can lose money in a hard year and be faithful in every particular. Those two are graded on the same scale and the market is not the scale. Then teach where the measurement is taken: "He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much: and he that is unjust in the least is unjust also in much" (Luke 16:10). Note the symmetry, because it is the part that convicts. The verse does not merely promote the faithful; it says the unjust in the least is ALREADY unjust in much. Present character, not future risk. There is no threshold at which a habit becomes serious; it was serious at the twenty dollars. The operational consequence is a discipline most managers resist: the routine, uninspected duties are the primary instrument. The returned call, the itemised deduction, the receipt kept, the repair logged the day it was done. An owner who reviews only totals is reading the least informative number available. And the pastoral note to say out loud: this is good news for the manager of a small portfolio. He is not waiting for scale to be tested. He is being tested now, on terms he can actually meet.',
