    levels: {
      teen: 'A man going on a trip hands his property to three workers. And he does not split it evenly. "And unto one he gave five talents, to another two, and to another one; to every man according to his several ability" (Matthew 25:15). Five, two, one. Measured to the person. So being handed less was never an insult, and being handed more was never a compliment. It was a fit. Two of them traded and doubled it. Now listen to what the owner says to the one who turned five into ten: "Well done, thou good and faithful servant: thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things" (Matthew 25:21). He says the exact same words to the one who turned two into four. Word for word. Same grade. Different amounts. Because the amount was never what was being graded. The third one buried his in the ground. And here is the part people miss. The owner does not say he should have earned more. He says: "Thou oughtest therefore to have put my money to the exchangers" (Matthew 25:27). The bank. The safest, easiest, least skilled thing available. He did not even do that. So the failure was not a bad trade. The failure was doing nothing at all. So ask what is buried in your hands right now. A room nobody has cleared out. A tool that needs one part. A job you keep almost starting. None of that is a crime, and that is exactly the point. The third worker did not steal anything. He did nothing, and nothing was the whole charge. Do the smallest safe thing today and you are already out of his column. One more thing worth seeing. The two who traded took a risk with somebody else’s money, and the owner was fine with it. Doing nothing looked like the safe choice and it was the only one that failed.',
      senior: 'Teach this parable as a management doctrine, because that is exactly what it is: an owner, absent, with assets in the hands of managers, and a reckoning at his return. Start with the allocation: "to every man according to his several ability" (Matthew 25:15). The distribution is unequal ON PURPOSE and calibrated to capacity. That settles two arguments at once. The manager handed less was not slighted, and the manager handed more was not flattered; each was measured. A portfolio sized to a man is a mercy, not a verdict. Then the commendation, and this is the line to slow down on: the servant who returned ten and the servant who returned four receive IDENTICAL words — "thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things" (Matthew 25:21). Even the man with five hears his charge called a FEW things. Scale is not the subject anywhere in the account. Now the third servant, who is usually taught as a coward and rarely taught precisely. The owner’s answer names a specific alternative: "Thou oughtest therefore to have put my money to the exchangers, and then at my coming I should have received mine own with usury" (Matthew 25:27). The exchangers are the lowest-effort, lowest-skill, lowest-risk option in the world he lived in. The charge is not a poor return; it is INACTION where even the minimum was available. Apply it without flinching. A unit held off the market because renting it is a hassle. A rent left under market for years because the conversation is awkward. A repair deferred until it compounds. A tenant question unanswered. Each of those is burial, and the standard is not brilliance — it is the exchangers: the smallest responsible action available now.',
    },
    lesson: 'Read the parable of the talents as what it plainly is: a management doctrine. An absent owner, real assets in the hands of managers, and a reckoning when he returns. THE ALLOCATION IS THE FIRST TEACHING. "And unto one he gave five talents, to another two, and to another one; to every man according to his several ability" (Matthew 25:15). Unequal on purpose, and calibrated to capacity. That settles two arguments before they start: the manager given less was not slighted, and the manager given more was not flattered. Each was measured. A portfolio sized to the man is a mercy rather than a verdict, and the manager who spends his energy resenting his allocation is already failing the only test that is running. THEN THE COMMENDATION, and this is the line to slow down on. The servant who returned ten and the servant who returned four hear the same sentence, word for word: "Well done, thou good and faithful servant: thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord" (Matthew 25:21). Identical. And notice that even the man entrusted with five hears his charge called "a few things". Scale is not the subject anywhere in this account — which is a relief if your portfolio is small and a warning if it is large. NOW THE THIRD SERVANT, who is often taught as a coward and rarely taught precisely. The owner does not tell him he should have earned more, and does not fault his skill. He names a specific alternative: "Thou oughtest therefore to have put my money to the exchangers, and then at my coming I should have received mine own with usury" (Matthew 25:27). The exchangers were the lowest-effort, lowest-skill, lowest-risk option available in that world — handing it to the bankers and doing nothing else. The charge is not a bad trade. The charge is INACTION where even the minimum was on the table. APPLY THAT TO PROPERTY WORK AND IT GETS UNCOMFORTABLE FAST, which is the point of a capstone. A unit held off the market because turning it over is a hassle. A rent left far under market for years because the conversation is awkward, until the correction has to be brutal. A repair deferred until it compounds into a replacement. A tenant’s question unanswered for a week. A vacancy nobody advertised. Every one of those is burial, and none of them feels like theft while you are doing it. The standard is not brilliance and it never was. The standard is the exchangers: the smallest responsible action actually available to you, taken now, on what you were measured and handed.',
