    bigIdea: 'Every steward gives an account, and Scripture says the account itself can be joyful or grievous: those who watch over others do it "as they that must give account, that they may do it with joy, and not with grief: for that is unprofitable for you" (Hebrews 13:17). Read the last clause — a grieved steward is unprofitable for THE PEOPLE HE CARRIES, not only for himself. And the faithful manager’s end is not a balance sheet: "Well done, thou good and faithful servant", and the invitation inside it: "enter thou into the joy of thy lord" (Matthew 25:21). The department closes where it opened — the title is His, and so is the joy.',
    inApp: 'Write the account you would give today on one property: what you were entrusted with, what you did, what you left undone, and who was served or not served. One page. Then decide whether it reads with joy or with grief — and fix the specific thing making it grief.',
    anchor: { ref: 'Hebrews 13:17; Matthew 25:21', theme: 'Every steward gives an account, and Scripture says the account itself can go two ways — those who watch over others do it "as they that must give account, that they may do it with joy, and not with grief." Note where the cost of a grieved steward lands: on the people he carries. And hear the welcome the faithful one receives: "Well done, thou good and faithful servant", with the invitation inside it, "enter thou into the joy of thy lord."' },
    levels: {
      teen: 'Everybody who runs something for somebody else has to give an account of it one day. That is not scary by itself. Here is the interesting part. Hebrews says the people who watch over others do it "as they that must give account, that they may do it with joy, and not with grief: for that is unprofitable for you" (Hebrews 13:17). So the account can go two ways. With joy, or with grief. And look at the last bit: "for that is unprofitable for YOU." A manager who ends up grieved is bad for the PEOPLE in his care, not just bad for himself. Your attitude is not your private business when other people depend on you. Now remember the servants in the talents story. The one who had done the work heard this: "Well done, thou good and faithful servant", and the invitation inside it: "enter thou into the joy of thy lord" (Matthew 25:21). The reward was not money. It was joy — and it was HIS lord’s joy, shared. So this whole department ends where it started. The land is His. The title is His. The house is His. And the joy at the end is His too, and He shares it with people who took care of what He handed them. That is what a steward is working toward. Not a bigger pile. A "well done" from the Owner. So try it now, on paper, while there is still time to change the answer. Write one page about something you look after. What were you given? What did you actually do? What did you leave undone? Who was helped, and who was not? Then read it back and ask one question: does this read like joy, or like something you would rather not hand in? Whatever makes it the second one, that is your work this week. Fix that. And hold on to the last part, because it is the best part of the whole course. The reward at the end is not a bigger pile. It is being told well done, and being let into your Master’s own joy.',
      senior: 'Close the department the way Scripture closes a stewardship: with the account. Hebrews 13:17 describes those who watch over others as men "that must give account, that they may do it with joy, and not with grief: for that is unprofitable for you." Three observations, and the third is the one people miss. The account is CERTAIN — it is not held out as a possibility. Its CHARACTER varies: joy or grief, and the difference is made long before the reckoning, by the record described in the previous seven lessons. And the final clause locates the cost of a grieved steward on the PEOPLE: "for that is unprofitable for you." A manager carrying resentment, exhaustion or concealment is a worse manager for the households in his care. So morale is not a private matter for anyone holding other people’s dwellings. This is the pastoral half of the trade, and it is in the text rather than imported from management literature. Then set the welcome beside it: "Well done, thou good and faithful servant: thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord" (Matthew 25:21). The final reward is not compensation. It is PARTICIPATION: entry into the owner’s own joy. The trade has no equivalent category for that. And note the department’s frame closing on itself: the first course began with Leviticus 25:23, the title kept by Yahweh, the holder a stranger and sojourner with Him. The account is given to that same Owner. So look at what this course has taught. Faithfulness measured at the least. Action rather than burial. Records an owner could pick up. A battlement built for a stranger. Provision in due season. Restitution with a date. None of that is a compliance programme. They are what a man does who expects to be glad at the reckoning, and who knows whose joy it is he is being invited into.',
    },
    lesson: 'A department that began with the title deed ends with the account, because that is how stewardship works in Scripture: what was entrusted gets reckoned. Hebrews describes those who watch over others as men "that must give account, that they may do it with joy, and not with grief: for that is unprofitable for you" (Hebrews 13:17). THREE THINGS IN THAT SENTENCE, and the third is the one usually missed. THE ACCOUNT IS CERTAIN. It is not offered as a possibility to prepare for; it is stated as a fact to be ready for. ITS CHARACTER VARIES — joy or grief — and the difference is not decided at the reckoning. It is decided in the seven lessons before this one: whether faithfulness was measured at the least, whether anything was buried that could have gone to the exchangers, whether the records are clean enough for an owner to pick up, whether the battlement was built for a stranger, whether the portion came in due season, whether anything lawful and invisible was being extracted from someone who could not refuse. AND THE LAST CLAUSE PUTS THE COST OF A GRIEVED STEWARD ON THE PEOPLE: "for that is unprofitable for you." Not unprofitable for him — for THEM. A manager carrying resentment, exhaustion, or something he is concealing is a worse manager for the households depending on him. Which means, for anyone holding other people’s dwellings, your own condition is not a private matter. That is the pastoral half of this trade, and it is in the text rather than borrowed from a management book. NOW SET THE WELCOME BESIDE IT. The servant who traded faithfully hears: "Well done, thou good and faithful servant: thou hast been faithful over a few things, I will make thee ruler over many things: enter thou into the joy of thy lord" (Matthew 25:21). The final reward is not compensation; it is PARTICIPATION — entry into the Owner’s own joy. This trade has no equivalent category, and that is worth saying out loud to a room full of people who have been taught to measure themselves in doors and dollars. AND SEE THE FRAME CLOSE. The first course of this department opened on Leviticus 25:23 — the land is His, the holder a stranger and a sojourner with Him. Everything since has been the working out of that one fact: a boundary that is a moral object, a price bounded by years, records kept as an act of hope, a cost counted to finish, a wage that cannot wait overnight, a successor formed and not merely named, faithfulness measured at the least, action instead of burial, a railing built for someone who has not arrived, provision in due season, restitution with a date on it. None of that is a compliance programme, and none of it was invented here. It is what a man does who expects to be GLAD at the reckoning — who knows the account is real, knows Whose it is, and knows exactly Whose joy he is being invited into.',
